## Students from the European Union

For **students who are nationals of the European Union (EU), the European Economic Area (EEA), or Switzerland**, tuition fees for the 2026–2027 academic year are:

- €178 per year for a Bachelor's degree program;
- €255 per year for a Master's degree program;
- €630 per year for an engineering degree program at an institution under the supervision of the French Ministry of Higher Education and Research, *except for programs offered by the* *Écoles Centrales (CentraleSupélec, Centrale Lille, Centrale Lyon, Centrale Méditerranée, and Centrale Nantes) and at Mines Nancy, where tuition fees are €2,620 per year*;
- €397 per year for a Doctoral (PhD) program.

### Students exempt from differentiated tuition fees

Certain international students from outside the European Union pay the same tuition fees as French, EU, EEA, and Swiss students, depending on their status or the program in which they are enrolled. This applies to:

- Students who are **residents of Québec**;
- International students holding a long-term **resident permit,** as well as students who have declared France as their tax residence, or who have been attached to a **French tax household, for more than two years**;
- Students who have been granted refugee status or subsidiary protection, as well as the children of individuals holding either status;
- International students enrolled in doctoral programs, the ***Habilitation à diriger des recherches (HDR)***, and **third-cycle medical**, dental, and pharmaceutical programs, with the exception of short-cycle programs;
- International students of any nationality enrolled in a *classe préparatoire aux grandes écoles (CPGE)*who are also concurrently enrolled in a Bachelor's degree program at a French university.

## List of public higher education institutions

Here is the list of the main public higher education institutions in France that fall under the authority of the Ministry of Higher Education, Research, and Space:

- All French universities and universities of technology, including the Université de Technologie de Belfort-Montbéliard, Université de Technologie de Compiègne, Université de Technologie de Tarbes Occitanie Pyrénées, and Université de Technologie de Troyes;
- Experimental public institutions (*établissements publics expérimentaux* – EPEs), grands établissements (such as CNAM, INALCO, and EHESS), and Communities of Universities and Institutions (*Communautés d’universités et établissements*– COMUEs);
- Schools and institutes operating independently of universities, including national engineering schools, the National Institutes of Applied Sciences (INSA Centre Val de Loire, INSA Hauts-de-France, INSA Lyon, INSA Rennes, INSA Rouen Normandie, INSA Strasbourg, and INSA Toulouse), and the Toulouse National Polytechnic Institute;
- The Écoles Normales Supérieures (ENS Paris-Saclay, ENS de Lyon, ENS Paris, and ENS Rennes);
- The Écoles Centrales (CentraleSupélec, Centrale Lille, Centrale Lyon, Centrale Méditerranée, and Centrale Nantes);

